# Gophenberg configuration. Copy to .env and fill in the values.
# Real environment variables take precedence over anything set here.
# Every command reads this file, not only serve, and every value is trimmed of surrounding spaces.

# The database, as a PostgreSQL address or as sqlite: and the absolute path of a SQLite file, such
# as sqlite:/srv/gophenberg/site.db. Create the empty file first. Only check, migrate and the
# account commands run on a SQLite file so far. serve and seed -yes stop with an error naming this
# setting. A PostgreSQL address takes no pool_ setting. Write every sign in its password that is
# not a letter or a digit as its % code, such as %40 for @ and %23 for #.
GOPHENBERG_DATABASE_URL=postgres://postgres:gophenberg@localhost:5435/postgres?sslmode=disable
# GOPHENBERG_ADDR=localhost:8081

# A private Unix socket to listen on in place of GOPHENBERG_ADDR, on Linux only. Its mode is 0600,
# so only this user's programs reach it. It needs GOPHENBERG_CLIENT_ADDR_HEADER, the header the
# program in front of it names the visitor in. It takes no GOPHENBERG_THEMES_DIR and no
# GOPHENBERG_INSTALL_ROOT yet. With neither a socket nor an address set, the site listens on every
# interface at the PORT its host names, else on localhost:8081.
# GOPHENBERG_SOCKET=/srv/gophenberg/run/gophenberg.sock
# GOPHENBERG_CLIENT_ADDR_HEADER=X-Client-Addr

# How long the site waits with nothing open before it exits with code 0, such as 15m. 0 or unset
# keeps it serving. A host that starts the site again at the next visit is the one place to set it.
# GOPHENBERG_IDLE_EXIT=15m

# A built admin folder to serve in place of the admin built into the binary. A build made with plain
# go build carries only a page saying no admin is built in, so point this at frontend/dist after
# pnpm --filter @gophenberg/frontend build. The folder must hold index.html, or serve and check stop.
# GOPHENBERG_WEB_DIR=frontend/dist

# How many connections to the database stay open at most, from 2 to 2147483647, the larger of 4
# and the number of CPUs when unset. On PostgreSQL the site and its plugins share one pool of this
# size. On a SQLite file each connection keeps its own page cache.
# GOPHENBERG_DATABASE_MAX_CONNS=4

# How many processors run Go code at once, from 1 to the number of CPUs, and the memory Go tries to
# stay under, in megabytes from 1 to 1073741824. Unset, Go picks the processors from the CPUs and the
# container's quota and sets no memory limit, unless GOMAXPROCS or GOMEMLIMIT is in the environment.
# Go reads those two before this file loads, so a line for them here does nothing. Set these two
# instead on a host where .env is the only way in, such as a small hosting plan. Each only ever
# lowers what Go holds. Every command hands them to Go, after this file for the commands that read
# it. A memory limit below what the site uses at rest makes Go collect garbage almost without pause.
# GOPHENBERG_MAX_PROCS=
# GOPHENBERG_MEMORY_LIMIT_MB=

# What the server does when it connects to PostgreSQL as a superuser, which can make the database
# server run programs: warn logs a warning and serves, refuse stops it. A SQLite file ignores it.
# GOPHENBERG_SUPERUSER_POLICY=warn

# How long serve waits for a PostgreSQL server that is not up yet, such as one still starting at
# boot, and the pause between two tries. Only a refused connection, a socket that is not there yet
# or a server still starting up is waited for. A wrong password, a missing role or a missing
# database stops serve at once. 0 does not wait. Only serve waits, every other command stops at once.
# GOPHENBERG_DATABASE_WAIT=60s
# GOPHENBERG_DATABASE_WAIT_INTERVAL=1s

# Only a sqlite: address reads these seven, and a PostgreSQL address ignores them. How long a
# connection waits for a lock another one holds, in whole milliseconds.
# GOPHENBERG_SQLITE_BUSY_TIMEOUT=5s
# How often opening a new connection tries the lock again, in whole milliseconds, up to the busy
# timeout. A busy timeout under 10ms lowers the default to match it. A longer poll tries less
# often, so opening a connection can wait up to one poll after the lock is free. Every other wait
# tries again on SQLite's own schedule, which slows down to one try every 100ms.
# GOPHENBERG_SQLITE_BUSY_POLL=10ms
# The page cache of each connection, in kibibytes.
# GOPHENBERG_SQLITE_CACHE_SIZE=2000
# full writes the log to disk at every commit. normal writes it only at each checkpoint, which is
# faster, but a power cut can lose the last commits.
# GOPHENBERG_SQLITE_SYNCHRONOUS=full
# The size in bytes the write-ahead log is cut back to after a checkpoint. Unset sets no limit.
# GOPHENBERG_SQLITE_JOURNAL_SIZE_LIMIT=67108864
# How long a migration waits for the lock file beside the database, and how often it tries again.
# GOPHENBERG_SQLITE_MIGRATE_LOCK_WAIT=30s
# GOPHENBERG_SQLITE_MIGRATE_LOCK_POLL=250ms

# The server the Go tests make their throwaway databases on, and how old a test database with no
# session must be before a test run drops it, as a Go duration above zero. Only go test uses these
# two, and the server loads them like every key here and ignores them. The database tests stop when
# either is missing or invalid, and CI sets both in its workflows. Leave out any pool_ setting,
# which a test database cannot take. Every checkout on the same server drops the stale databases
# of all of them, so keep the age above the longest test run of any checkout. The tests also cap
# each test database handle at GOPHENBERG_DATABASE_MAX_CONNS above, read from the environment first
# and this file second.
GOPHENBERG_TEST_DATABASE_URL=postgres://postgres:gophenberg@localhost:5435/postgres?sslmode=disable
GOPHENBERG_TEST_DATABASE_MAX_AGE=1h

# Name the public site carries in its chrome. Defaults to Gophenberg.
# GOPHENBERG_SITE_TITLE=Gophenberg

# GOPHENBERG_FEED_TITLE=Gophenberg
# GOPHENBERG_FEED_ITEMS=20

# Plugins share the site's database pool. Each plugin adds this many slots to the share,
# one slot for each statement, open result set or transaction, from 1 to 1000.
# GOPHENBERG_PLUGIN_DB_CONNS=4
# How many of the pool's connections the share always leaves to the site itself, from 1 to 1000.
# The share never holds more slots than GOPHENBERG_DATABASE_MAX_CONNS less this reserve, and the
# server does not start when that leaves the plugins none, so a cap of 2 needs a reserve of 1.
# GOPHENBERG_PLUGIN_DB_RESERVE=2
# How long one plugin statement may run, its wait for a slot included. It also ends a plugin's read
# of account names.
# GOPHENBERG_PLUGIN_QUERY_TIMEOUT=5s
# How long one plugin transaction may stay open.
# GOPHENBERG_PLUGIN_TX_TIMEOUT=30s

# How the site draws avatars, as the Avatars part of WordPress's Discussion settings does. initials
# draws the first letter of the name on the site and sends nothing out. gravatar shows the picture
# Gravatar keeps for the email address, and sends Gravatar a hash of that address. off shows none.
# The admin's author column and every plugin follow it.
# GOPHENBERG_AVATARS=initials
# The picture Gravatar shows for an email address it does not know: mystery, blank,
# gravatar_default, identicon, wavatar, monsterid, retro, robohash or color. WordPress's
# Initials choice is refused, since it sends letters of each name to Gravatar.
# GOPHENBERG_AVATAR_DEFAULT=mystery
# The most mature Gravatar picture an avatar may show: G, PG, R or X.
# GOPHENBERG_AVATAR_RATING=G

# Cron jobs are work that runs on a rhythm, such as sweeping expired sessions. The first visit
# after a job falls due runs it, once the page is answered. The host's cron can run them too, with
# POST /api/cron and the secret in an Authorization: Bearer header. The secret takes at least
# 32 characters, such as the output of openssl rand -hex 32. Empty leaves that address off.
# GOPHENBERG_CRON_SECRET=
# Whether a visit may start the cron jobs. Turn it off only when the host's cron calls the address.
# GOPHENBERG_CRON_ON_VISITS=true
# How long after one check a visit waits before it checks again.
# GOPHENBERG_CRON_VISIT_PACE=1m
# How long one job run may take before it is cut short.
# GOPHENBERG_CRON_TIME_LIMIT=30s
# How long a run holds its job so no other run takes it. It must stand above the time limit.
# It is also the longest the server waits for the database to list or hand over a job.
# GOPHENBERG_CRON_LEASE=1m
# How long a stop gives a running job to end and record itself, before the plugins stop.
# GOPHENBERG_CRON_STOP_GRACE=2s
# How often expired sessions and expired account links are swept.
# GOPHENBERG_SESSION_SWEEP_INTERVAL=1h

# Comma-separated CIDR ranges of reverse proxies allowed to set X-Forwarded-* headers.
# Behind a TLS-terminating proxy this must list it, or public links are built as http.
# GOPHENBERG_TRUSTED_PROXIES=10.0.0.0/8

# The address people reach the site at. When set, a write sent to any other address is refused.
# GOPHENBERG_PUBLIC_URL=https://example.com

# Where themes are installed, one directory per theme. Admin uploads write here, so any mount
# has to stay writable. The image sets /themes and expects a volume there.
# GOPHENBERG_THEMES_DIR=/srv/gophenberg/themes

# Pins one theme, overriding the admin. Empty lets the admin choose which theme serves.
# GOPHENBERG_THEME=starter

# Where uploaded media is stored, under year and month directories. Uploads write here and the
# public site serves the files from it, so any mount has to stay writable and has to survive a
# restart. Unset, the media library refuses every upload. The image sets /media and expects a
# volume there.
# GOPHENBERG_MEDIA_DIR=/srv/gophenberg/media

# The node binary a theme is served with. Defaults to node on PATH, and the image sets it to the
# node it ships at /nodejs/bin/node.
# GOPHENBERG_NODE_BIN=node

# The largest upload the media library takes, in megabytes.
# GOPHENBERG_MEDIA_UPLOAD_CAP_MB=128

# How many uploaded images the server decodes and resizes at once, from 1 to 64. The rest
# wait their turn.
# GOPHENBERG_MEDIA_CONCURRENT_IMAGES=2

# The most pixels a picture or one frame of a GIF may hold, in millions. A picture takes far more
# memory once decoded than its file takes, about 1.5 MB per megapixel for a baseline JPEG photo,
# 4 MB for a PNG, 7.5 MB for a progressive JPEG, 8 MB for a 16-bit PNG and up to 15 MB for a
# progressive JPEG with full colour detail, so a small host names a lower budget. A picture past
# the budget is refused.
# GOPHENBERG_MEDIA_MAX_MEGAPIXELS=80

# How long a media or theme upload has to arrive, in place of the HTTP read timeout.
# GOPHENBERG_UPLOAD_TIMEOUT=5m

# The largest definitions file an import takes, in kilobytes, up to 1024.
# GOPHENBERG_DEFINITIONS_IMPORT_CAP_KB=256

# How many containers a field may stand inside, from 1 to 1000. A flexible content layout counts
# as one.
# GOPHENBERG_FIELD_DEPTH=32

# The page sizes an admin list offers, from 2 to 6 of them, each once from the smallest up and none
# past the page cap, 10,20,50,100 when unset. The size a list opens at, one of those sizes, 20 when
# unset. The most items one admin list page carries, 100 when unset, a larger request answered at it.
# The size the media picker opens at, one of those sizes, 50 when unset and the sizes offer it, the
# size a list opens at when they do not.
# GOPHENBERG_LIST_PAGE_SIZES=10,20,50,100
# GOPHENBERG_LIST_PAGE_SIZE=20
# GOPHENBERG_LIST_PAGE_CAP=100
# GOPHENBERG_PICKER_PAGE_SIZE=50

# How long a confirmation toast stays on screen, in whole milliseconds from 1ms to 2147483647ms.
# The first is a toast with no button, 6s when unset. The second is a toast with a button, which
# only a plugin raises today, 10s when unset.
# GOPHENBERG_TOAST_DURATION=6s
# GOPHENBERG_TOAST_ACTION_DURATION=10s

# The locale the admin writes dates, times and numbers in, whatever the interface language. A BCP 47
# tag that names a language, es-ES when unset, which writes 04/10/2026, 14:05 and 1.234,56.
# GOPHENBERG_FORMAT_LOCALE=es-ES

# The role the Add User screen starts on, as WordPress's New User Default Role does, member when
# unset. A role neither the core nor a plugin declares stops the server at start.
# GOPHENBERG_DEFAULT_ROLE=member

# How long a starting theme has to answer before it is given up on, and how many times it is tried
# again. Raise the timeout for a theme that boots slowly on modest hardware.
# GOPHENBERG_THEME_READY_TIMEOUT=30s
# GOPHENBERG_THEME_START_ATTEMPTS=5

# How long to wait before retrying a theme that will not start, doubling after each attempt up to
# the maximum.
# GOPHENBERG_THEME_BACKOFF=500ms
# GOPHENBERG_THEME_MAX_BACKOFF=30s

# How long a theme has to stop before it is killed, and how long a running theme has to start
# answering one request.
# GOPHENBERG_THEME_STOP_GRACE=3s
# GOPHENBERG_THEME_PROXY_TIMEOUT=10s

# How long a browser may keep a site asset or an uploaded file. Behind a proxy that sets its own
# Cache-Control the proxy wins for assets.
# GOPHENBERG_CACHE_ASSET_MAX_AGE=1h
# GOPHENBERG_CACHE_MEDIA_MAX_AGE=1h

# How long a shared cache may serve a public content answer, and how much longer it may serve the
# old one while fetching a fresh one. Write whole seconds, like 1h or 90s.
# GOPHENBERG_CACHE_CONTENT_SHARED_MAX_AGE=1m
# GOPHENBERG_CACHE_CONTENT_STALE_WHILE_REVALIDATE=5m

# How long a browser may keep an admin file whose name carries its content hash. A new build gives a
# changed file a new name, so the window can be long. The admin page itself is checked on every load.
# GOPHENBERG_CACHE_ADMIN_ASSET_MAX_AGE=8760h

# How long a visitor has to send a request's headers and the whole request, and how long an idle
# connection waits for its next request.
# GOPHENBERG_HTTP_READ_HEADER_TIMEOUT=10s
# GOPHENBERG_HTTP_READ_TIMEOUT=30s
# GOPHENBERG_HTTP_IDLE_TIMEOUT=2m

# How long a stop gives running requests to finish, how long the ones cancelled after that get to
# end, and how long the plugins get to stop. Whatever stops the server must wait longer than these
# three and the theme stop grace added together. The plugin stop grace also bounds the plugins' stop
# after any other command that loads them, such as migrate.
# GOPHENBERG_SHUTDOWN_GRACE=10s
# GOPHENBERG_SHUTDOWN_CANCEL_GRACE=5s
# GOPHENBERG_SHUTDOWN_STOP_GRACE=5s

# How long an account command has to store the record of a change it applied, and how many records
# account:records lists when its -limit flag names no other.
# GOPHENBERG_COMMAND_RECORD_TIMEOUT=5s
# GOPHENBERG_COMMAND_RECORDS_LIMIT=50

# Where and how often the site checks for a newer Gophenberg. A check installs nothing. False stops
# the checks the site makes on its own, while update:check and the admin still check when asked.
# The address must be https, or http on a loopback host. The trusted keys add P-256 root keys, as
# Multikeys separated by commas, for an update site other than the official one, and are refused
# beside it. That site's key list must name a timestamp key, and the site must serve a timestamp
# file that has not expired, made with releasetool timestamp. The screen check age is how long the
# admin waits after one check before another. The Updates screen asks for the progress of an update
# it started at the progress interval, and waits for the site to come back after the update for the
# restart wait, both in whole milliseconds up to 2147483647ms.
# GOPHENBERG_UPDATE_CHECKS=true
# GOPHENBERG_UPDATE_CHECK_INTERVAL=12h
# GOPHENBERG_UPDATE_URL=https://updates.gophenberg.org
# GOPHENBERG_UPDATE_CHANNEL=stable
# GOPHENBERG_TRUSTED_KEYS=
# GOPHENBERG_UPDATE_TIMEOUT=30s
# GOPHENBERG_UPDATES_SCREEN_CHECK_AGE=1m
# GOPHENBERG_UPDATES_SCREEN_PROGRESS_INTERVAL=2s
# GOPHENBERG_UPDATES_SCREEN_RESTART_WAIT=5m

# How a server install updates itself with update:apply or from the admin. The install root is an
# absolute path that holds each build side by side, with a current link naming the one that runs.
# Empty, as in the Docker image, means the site only tells you about updates. An update started from
# the admin stops the server with exit code 75 once it switched builds, so run the server under a
# service manager that starts it again, such as systemd with Restart=always. The cap and the
# timeout bound the download of the release archive. The margin is the free space an update asks
# for beyond the cap and the backup. The backups folder keeps the newest backups, taken with the
# pg_dump named here, whose major version must be at least the server's. At its first starts the
# new build checks itself, and it goes back to the build before it once it tried to start more
# times than the attempts allow or the server does not answer its check in time. A build that
# passes keeps the newest builds, as many as the versions setting names, and the build the
# previous link names.
# GOPHENBERG_INSTALL_ROOT=
# GOPHENBERG_UPDATE_DOWNLOAD_CAP_MB=200
# GOPHENBERG_UPDATE_DOWNLOAD_TIMEOUT=10m
# GOPHENBERG_UPDATE_DISK_MARGIN_MB=64
# GOPHENBERG_UPDATE_BACKUPS_KEEP=3
# GOPHENBERG_PG_DUMP_BIN=pg_dump
# GOPHENBERG_UPDATE_ATTEMPTS=3
# GOPHENBERG_UPDATE_VERIFY_TIMEOUT=1m
# GOPHENBERG_UPDATE_VERSIONS_KEEP=2

# Credentials for the translation platform, read by make translations and make translations-retire.
# The scheduled workflow takes these from repository secrets instead.
# POEDITOR_API_TOKEN=
# POEDITOR_PROJECT_ID=
